Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server wrapping a REST API:

---

**Program.cs** (stdio transport):

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

builder.Services.AddHttpClient("WeatherApi", client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.weather.example.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

**WeatherTools.cs** (tool class wrapping a REST API):

```csharp
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(IHttpClientFactory httpFactory, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get the current weather forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name, e.g. 'Seattle'")] string city,
        [Description("Units: 'metric' or 'imperial'")] string units = "metric",
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching forecast for {City}", city);
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        var response = await client.GetStringAsync(
            $"/forecast?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}&units={units}",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Look up coordinates (lat/lon) for a city name.")]
    public async Task<string> GeoLookup(
        [Description("City name to geocode")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        var client = httpFactory.CreateClient("WeatherApi");
        var response = await client.GetStringAsync(
            $"/geo?city={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK resolves dependencies **automatically** — no manual wiring:

| Pattern | How it works |
|---------|--------------|
| **Constructor injection** (non-static class) | The class is instantiated per-request; DI services go in the primary constructor. Register services in `Program.cs` (`AddHttpClient`, `AddSingleton`, etc.) |
| **Method parameter injection** (static class) | DI services can appear directly as method parameters. The SDK detects registered types and injects them — they're hidden from the tool schema |
| **Framework types** | `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>` are always injected automatically |

**Key point:** Only parameters with `[Description]` are exposed to the LLM. Everything else (DI services, `CancellationToken`, etc.) is invisible in the schema.

---

To scaffold this yourself:

```bash
dotnet new mcpserver -n WeatherMcpServer
cd WeatherMcpServer
dotnet add package Microsoft.Extensions.Http
dotnet build && dotnet run
```

The server will start and wait for JSON-RPC messages on stdin. Connect it from VS Code, GitHub Copilot, or any MCP client by pointing at the binary as a subprocess.
